WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.480
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.400
If Sue runs around the track twice,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.820
and John runs around the track 4 times more than Sue,

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.000
How many times does John run around the track?

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:17.780
This problem caused some confusion after it was assigned to 3rd graders.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.600
The teacher was marking one number as the correct answer

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.060
and a parent believed another number was the correct answer.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.160
The problem was then posted online,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.920
and many people believe the correct answer was a third number!

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.920
So what's the correct answer?

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.520
In this  video, I'm going to present 3 different ways to interpret this problem,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.820
and suggest what is the correct answer according to modern usage.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.540
The heart of the confusion is the phrase "four times more than".

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:58.380
Typically, this phrase refers to multiplication.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.840
"times more" means that John runs around the track

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.040
four times more than the number of times that Sue runs.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.840
So we take 4 times 2 to get to the answer of 8.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.880
Other people had a different interpretation of this problem.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:28.440
They read this as saying "John runs around four times more" as in addition.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.480
So if Sue runs around the track 2 times,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.800
John runs around 4 more laps than Sue.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.460
This is 4 more times.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.340
So the number of times that John would run would be 4 plus 2, which equals 6.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.520
The problem with this interpretation is we usually write "More times"

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.400
when we're referring to addition.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.920
And when we have the phrase "times more" we're usually referring to multiplication.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.940
There's a third way to read this problem which would be literally correct

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.400
according to these words on a strict grammatical basis.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.440
The literal version of this problem is often used in terms of percentages.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.760
"Times more than" would mean you should add that number of times to the base.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.840
So think about this in terms of percentages.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:33.640
100% more than 2 is a total of 4.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.200
200% more than 2 is a total of 6.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.180
300% more than 2 is a total of 8,

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.940
and 400% more than 2 is a total of 10.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.420
So 400% more is the same thing as 4 times.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.120
So having 4 times more than Sue,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.320
would mean 4 times 2 plus 2, which would equal 10 times.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.320
So this is an interpretation that is literally correct,

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.440
according to the way the sentence is written.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.720
However, in common usage,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.020
"4 times more" is the phrase that is commonly used in multiplication,

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.580
to mean 4 times larger.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.880
You'll see this in newspaper every single day.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.080
A more literal and correct way to refer to multiplication,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.080
would be writing "4 times as much as".

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.480
And you'll see that many writers use the phrase "times as much as"

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.700
to mean multiplication. It's a very strict way of writing it.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.560
So I would caution you against using this literal interpretation unless you see percentages.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.880
So in conclusion, of these three interpretations,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.520
I would say the correct answer is 8.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.320
because "times more" usually refers to multiplication,

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.940
and even "times more than" refers to multiplication.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.000
So if John runs 4 times more than Sue,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:06.860
the correct answer to this problem would be he would run 4 times 2 which is 8 times around the track.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.140
What do you think the correct answer should be?

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.180
Let us know in the comments.

